  <data key="d5">1791642325</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="mathematical expression">
  <data key="d0">mathematical expression</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dded67fd8182de2201aa35ccbb4c8cd9</data>
  <data key="d2">Multimodal Knowledge Unification converts mathematical expressions into symbolic representations.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642326</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="x&lt;sub&gt;j&lt;/sub&gt;">
  <data key="d0">x&lt;sub&gt;j&lt;/sub&gt;</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dded67fd8182de2201aa35ccbb4c8cd9</data>
  <data key="d2">Multimodal Knowledge Unification ensures that the resulting x&lt;sub&gt;j&lt;/sub&gt; preserves both content and structural context within the source.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642326</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structurally Complex Real-World Content">
  <data key="d0">Structurally Complex Real-World Content</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18ade91e917d2c67b94279ae6220dd06</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-conventional processing strategies are required for documents with complex structures.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642327</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structurally Ambiguous Table">
  <data key="d0">Structurally Ambiguous Table</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18ade91e917d2c67b94279ae6220dd06</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 12 illustrates the structural ambiguity in a table layout.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642328</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decomposition of Knowledge Source into Atomic Units (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Decomposition of Knowledge Source into Atomic Units (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation \( k_{i} \xrightarrow{\text{Decompose}} \{ c_{j} = (t_{j}, x_{j}) \}_{j=1}^{n_{i}} \) represents the process of breaking down a complex knowledge source into atomic content units, each consisting of a modality type and its raw content. This is a critical step in the RAG-Anything framework for handling diverse and multimodal information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a9db98ce0de085186d020ad648b70ae3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="RAG-Anything Framework Architecture Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">RAG-Anything Framework Architecture Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a high-level architectural diagram of the RAG-Anything framework. It illustrates the end-to-end process of handling heterogeneous multimodal documents. The pipeline starts with parsing various file formats, unifies them into a structured content list, constructs dual knowledge graphs (text-based and cross-modal), and merges them with a vector database. Finally, it shows how a user query is processed through hybrid retrieval (structural and semantic) to generate a response via an LLM. This figure is central to understanding the system's design and workflow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1025c419093621f01b9bd6bda58a4d8b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Graph Construction from Multimodal Content (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Graph Construction from Multimodal Content (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation \((\mathcal{V}_j, \mathcal{E}_j) = R(d_j^{chunk})\) formalizes the process of constructing a graph from a chunk of multimodal content in the RAG-Anything framework. It ensures that non-textual information is properly contextualized and integrated, addressing the limitations of existing RAG systems that primarily handle text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abcce11b032cb7a3594b3a1302046124</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Entity Node Set Construction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Entity Node Set Construction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation \(\tilde{V} = \{ v_{j}^{mm} \}_{j} \cup \bigcup_{j} \mathcal{V}_{j}\) defines the set of nodes in the multimodal knowledge graph, ensuring that both multimodal entity nodes and intra-chunk entities are included. This construction is essential for the RAG-Anything framework to provide a comprehensive and context-aware representation of the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f0dae3e849332dc4c4fecee687039a4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Knowledge Graph Edge Construction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Knowledge Graph Edge Construction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation defines the set of edges in the multimodal knowledge graph, ensuring that both intra-chunk entity relations and hierarchical relationships are captured. This is crucial for the accurate representation and retrieval of multimodal knowledge within the RAG-Anything framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0874d64a853fa0a8b53c1b2c9a950a9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Unified Embedding Table (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Unified Embedding Table (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation \(\mathcal{T} = \mathrm{emb}(s) : s \in \mathcal{V} \cup \mathcal{E} \cup c_{j_j}\) defines the process of generating dense vector representations for the unified knowledge graph in the RAG-Anything framework. This ensures that all elements, including entities, relationships, and atomic content chunks, are represented in a consistent vector space, facilitating efficient cross-modal retrieval and reasoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77ff6101b55afb188a8b2aba9f83cd92</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Experimental Datasets Statistics Table (table)">
  <data key="d0">Experimental Datasets Statistics Table (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">The table details the statistics of two experimental datasets, DocBench and MMLongBench, used to evaluate the RAG-Anything framework. DocBench contains 229 documents with an average of 66 pages and 46,377 tokens, while MMLongBench includes 135 documents with an average of 47.5 pages and 21,214 tokens. The datasets cover a wide range of document types and questions, providing a comprehensive testbed for the framework's performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a534f8987f70ae30ffc6f998509f96a7</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Response Generation Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Response Generation Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation \(\mathrm{Response} = \mathrm{VLM}(q, \mathcal{P}(q), \mathcal{V}^{\star}(q))\) defines the process of generating a response using a Vision-Language Model, integrating query, textual context, and visual content. This equation is central to the hybrid knowledge retrieval and synthesis framework, ensuring comprehensive and contextually rich answers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9cf50ce7fb3ca592afa7f4959b496cfe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Comparison Table on DocBench Dataset (table)">
  <data key="d0">Performance Comparison Table on DocBench Dataset (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares the performance of RAG-Anything and other methods on the DocBench dataset, highlighting RAG-Anything's superior overall and domain-specific accuracy. The table supports the claim that RAG-Anything's dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism are particularly effective for long-document reasoning tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a411e34e853ae45bfd9bcee5b30fdc44</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Accuracy Table (table)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Accuracy Table (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares the accuracy of different methods (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAG-Anything) on the MMLongBench dataset across various document domains and overall performance. RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance, particularly in long-context documents, supporting the claims about its effectiveness in handling complex multimodal information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dfc6c28e224a59948d1c3f970cd333d3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Accuracy vs Page Range Comparison (image)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Accuracy vs Page Range Comparison (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A line chart comparing the accuracy of RAGAnything and MMGraphRAG on the DocBench dataset across different document page ranges. It demonstrates that while both models perform similarly on short documents, RAGAnything significantly outperforms MMGraphRAG on documents exceeding 100 pages, supporting the paper's claim of superior long-context performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3199f24e41812cb62bea94f7c85f2636</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench QA Counts Distribution Chart (image)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench QA Counts Distribution Chart (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A bar chart illustrating the distribution of QA pairs in the DocBench dataset by document page range. It shows a high concentration of QA pairs in short documents (1-50 pages) and a decreasing count for longer documents (51+ pages), providing the dataset composition context for the performance analysis in the paper.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55d457152e0dea0c28144e5e94403cb2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Accuracy Comparison Chart (image)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Accuracy Comparison Chart (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A line chart comparing the accuracy of RAGAnything and MMGraphRAG on the MMLongBench dataset across five page range categories. The chart illustrates that RAGAnything outperforms MMGraphRAG, particularly on longer documents (101-200 and 200+ pages), supporting the paper's claim of superior long-context performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b7a22ef18d1df90b09c6752fdaa849</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Distribution of QA Pairs by Document Page Range (image)">
  <data key="d0">Distribution of QA Pairs by Document Page Range (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A bar chart showing the frequency of QA pairs categorized by document length. The data indicates a strong concentration of QA pairs in documents ranging from 11 to 50 pages, with significantly fewer pairs in shorter or longer documents. This figure supports the performance comparison section by illustrating the dataset composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-841b2e1a62bf11ef382f155dc5fd836b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Ablation Study Results Table (table)">
  <data key="d0">Ablation Study Results Table (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table presents ablation study results on the DocBench dataset, comparing the performance of 'Chunk-only', 'w/o Reranker', and 'RAGAnything' methods. The results demonstrate that the RAG-Anything framework, which integrates dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval, outperforms traditional chunk-based retrieval methods, especially in long-context and multimodal document scenarios. This supports the document's claims about the importance of structured context and multimodal information for accurate document reasoning tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c1dc026d368316a0e447d9d04cb5bc4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Comparison of model responses on multi-panel figure interpretation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Comparison of model responses on multi-panel figure interpretation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A case study figure comparing the performance of GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAG-Anything on a query regarding a multi-panel t-SNE visualization. The image highlights the specific evidence in a document (DAE style space) and shows that only RAG-Anything correctly identifies the DAE model as having clearer separation, while the others incorrectly select the VAE model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d7b65a77beeebaa7ba6c82a6b282bc8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Financial Table Navigation Case Study Comparison (image)">
  <data key="d0">Financial Table Navigation Case Study Comparison (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A comparative visualization demonstrating RAG-Anything's superior performance in financial table navigation. It shows a query about Novo Nordisk's 2020 wages, highlighting the correct evidence (26,778 DKK million) in a table. RAG-Anything correctly identifies this value, while GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, and MMGraphRAG provide incorrect answers, illustrating the system's ability to handle complex document structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3030640e3aa96c21a21f63f845339bb7</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Document Type Distribution Table (table)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Document Type Distribution Table (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table provides a detailed breakdown of document types and their associated statistics for the DocBench benchmark, highlighting the diversity and complexity of the documents used in the evaluation of RAG systems. It supports the evaluation of RAG systems across different document types and their characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-19c485068d1b3af6c308fbeb4976b40e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Document Type Distribution Table (table)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Document Type Distribution Table (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table details the distribution of document types and their associated statistics from the MMLongBench benchmark, providing a comprehensive view of the document types and their characteristics, which is crucial for evaluating the performance of multimodal retrieval-augmented generation systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6489e2fb8e8849e9fba34fe5f95e4a0f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Comparison of RAG systems on GCAN ablation chart query (image)">
  <data key="d0">Comparison of RAG systems on GCAN ablation chart query (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image compares the performance of GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAG-Anything in answering a question about a GCAN ablation study. The question asks which component removal caused the lowest accuracy on Twitter15. The image shows the source document, the relevant bar chart (Figure 4), and the four system responses. RAG-Anything is the only system to correctly identify the '-S-A' configuration as the lowest performer, while the others provide incomplete or incorrect answers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc6b663130fa82a15ae0568ebdd7e714</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Comparison of RAG systems on tabular navigation task (image)">
  <data key="d0">Comparison of RAG systems on tabular navigation task (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A figure comparing the performance of GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAG-Anything on a question regarding the highest AUPRC value in a complex table. RAG-Anything is shown to be the only system to correctly identify the value 0.506 for the 'GloVe + LSTM - Attention' model, while others fail due to structural ambiguity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-514397fe4b91c504d493b81ae4f2af0d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Vision Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Vision Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of the 'vision_analysis_prompt.png' file showing a structured JSON-based prompt template. It instructs an AI model to analyze images by providing detailed descriptions and entity information, incorporating surrounding context. This figure demonstrates the specific prompting strategy used in the RAG-Anything system for multimodal analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d13b5eb03f64e659615c9a7b0a7b167</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Table Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Table Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image shows the 'Table Analysis Prompt' template used in the RAG-Anything framework. It is a structured instruction set designed to guide a language model in analyzing tabular data. The prompt requests a JSON output containing a detailed description of the table's structure, data points, and statistical insights, along with an entity summary. It emphasizes the importance of context, requiring the model to relate the table's content to surrounding information. This prompt is part of the 'Context-Aware Multimodal Prompting' section, specifically designed for handling complex tabular structures as described in the surrounding text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d68c9b009fe16eb1ecc9d4a748e081f2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Equation Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of a text file containing a structured prompt template for analyzing mathematical equations. The prompt instructs an AI to provide a JSON response with detailed descriptions and entity information, including mathematical meaning, context, and significance. It includes placeholders for context, equation text, and format, emphasizing the use of specific terminology and connection to broader frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7f162fe747d4504218a5bc094c97cc4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Accuracy Evaluation Prompt for RAG System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Accuracy Evaluation Prompt for RAG System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of a text file containing a standardized prompt for evaluating the factual accuracy of RAG system outputs. The prompt instructs an AI evaluator to compare a generated answer against an expected answer, using a binary 0/1 accuracy scale and providing a JSON output with reasoning. It prioritizes factual correctness over style and handles edge cases like partial matches and unanswerable questions. This figure illustrates the methodology used for consistent assessment across the experimental framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63a71b30187cb0788470f7a4ef8b5a7a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Comparison of RAG system responses to a multimodal document query (image)">
  <data key="d0">Comparison of RAG system responses to a multimodal document query (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image compares the performance of four RAG systems (GPT-4o-mini, MIMGraphRAG, LightRAG, RAG-Anything) in answering a question about a neural network diagram found in a multimodal document. It highlights that RAG-Anything correctly identifies the data flow sequence from the diagram, while the other systems provide incorrect answers, demonstrating RAG-Anything's superior ability to interpret complex visual information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-26f9e861c68e93709074930b8e53d41c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-modal noise and ambiguous table structure failure case (image)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-modal noise and ambiguous table structure failure case (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates a failure case in multimodal RAG systems, where all tested methods (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, RAG-Anything) incorrectly answer a question about a table in a multimodal document. The question asks about style space separation, but the systems retrieve and report data on 'Joint goal accuracy' from an irregular table, demonstrating cross-modal misalignment and difficulty with ambiguous table structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e67b53472cda54f4238a2ccf98da3bdd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791642748</data>
</node>
